Kent Paul Dolan wrote:
> Are there other whole aspects of the scoring I've
> missed? Suggested new categories for discussion?

Yes, I would like to posit that the existing system isn't as broken as you
make out, and is even rather good.

For beginners, NetHack is about killing monsters and delving into dungeons,
both of which are rewarded adequately. I remember when I felt a real sense
of accomplishment in getting a score above 30,000 for the first time. 

For intermediate players, the only score that counts is whether you've
ascended or not. NetHack essentially scores as follows: 1 point for an
ascension, 0 points for anything else. It is a harsh mistress and we like
it that way.

For advanced players, there are conducts, ascension streaks, ascension
percentages, low turn counts, low kill counts, and, ironically, low score
counts. All of these are well-defined metrics of success, along the lines
of your multiple high score table proposal. NAO and the tournaments, for
example, have demonstrated how advanced players can compete with each other
in the absence of a meaningful integer score.

Maybe there is a better way to allocate points in the mid-game, but I think
it would entail an extremely complex model of the game and for all
practical purposes fail to be significantly better than what we have.

